SPRIND’s Next Frontier AI offers €125M equity-free funding for up to 10 teams and 3 labs, aiming for €1B follow-on each, providing a major boost to Europe’s AI ecosystem. Researchers and builders are encouraged to apply.

Timeline: Applications open on 30 April and the application deadline is 29 May.

@SPRIND: "Europe has world-class AI talent. By 2028, it will have three new Frontier AI labs. Applications for Next Frontier AI open in 15 days. Tag the strongest AI researcher or team you know in the comments."

Trent MCP is live for Claude Code, allowing users to run security assessments directly from their chat window. Trent scans your codebase, builds a prioritized remediation plan, and tasks feed directly into Claude Code.

@TrentAIHQ: "Security assessments directly inside Claude Code. Multiple agents in a continuous loop — scan, judge, mitigate, evaluate, right inside the chat. Each coding session leaves the project more secure than the last." 

The linked service, Trent AI, assesses your application's architecture within Claude Code, ensuring that not just the code, but the overall security of what you're building is evaluated.

The classification problem is one that has been quietly underestimated. Once a system can decide and act, the governance burden becomes qualitatively different, starting with the need to know which of your systems have crossed the line.

@ZhenwenDai: "The Autonomy Test in the ASMM is the part I'd point a new team at first. Not because it's the technical core, but because everything else in the framework only makes sense once you know which systems you're scoring. Most teams haven't drawn that line yet."

The AI Security Maturity Model (ASMM) helps assess and improve the security maturity of agentic AI systems, aligning with standards like NIST and the EU AI Act.
